---
import { STUDIO_EMAIL } from '../lib/copy';

/**
 * DESIGN.md §B.10 — `position: sticky; bottom: 0` on an element inside a wrapper
 * spanning from the end of the hero to the start of the final CTA. It appears
 * when the hero leaves, follows down the page, and retires on its own when the
 * real CTA arrives. No JS, no scroll listener, no dismiss state.
 *
 * QUESTIONS.md item 53: the plate prints the address itself, not a label.
 *
 * §B.10's address table is per world, and QUESTIONS.md item 66 confirmed it: the studio
 * pages carry `thegeekdogs@gmail.com`, `/sahib/` carries his and `/tanya/` hers. The
 * plate and the page never disagree about who is being written to, which is the whole
 * reason the person pages get their own addresses at all. The component does not change
 * width between them: set at 14px Instrument 600 with 20px of padding each side, the
 * longest of the three needs 209px of the 260 available (§B.10).
 *
 * COPY.md §1 gives one accessible-name pattern for all three, with the page's own
 * address substituted, so the spoken name and the printed label never name different
 * people.
 *
 * **Below 768 the plate does not render, and that is a copy blocker rather than a
 * decision.** §B.10 round 10 withdrew the 260px address plate at that width on measured
 * evidence — at 360 it covered 236 of the card panel's 280px measure, 84% of the line —
 * and replaced it with a 112 x 56 plate carrying a LABEL rather than the address, since
 * 112 with its padding leaves ~73px of type against the 169px the shortest address
 * needs. Both of that label's strings are `[COPY NEEDED]` in §B.10 and neither is in
 * COPY.md: not the <= 9-character label, not the <= 8-word accessible name that has to
 * speak the address the plate links to. An amber box with nothing in it and no
 * accessible name is not a smaller failure than the one round 10 fixed, so the element
 * is held until the strings land, exactly as the strip's row links and §B.8's pipeline
 * label were held until COPY.md carried them.
 *
 * Nothing is lost that is not recoverable: §B.10's own note is that below 768 what the
 * plate gives up is being THE printed proof, and the address still prints in the page's
 * final CTA, three times on /contact/, and in each person page's closing block. The
 * 112px width and the 72px reserve are already in `tokens.css`, and §B.10's composition
 * rule is honoured at the narrower 128px band, so the day the two strings arrive this
 * is one `display` declaration.
 */
interface Props {
  address?: string;
}

const { address = STUDIO_EMAIL } = Astro.props;
---

<a
  class="plate"
  data-cta="contact-plate"
  href={`mailto:${address}`}
  aria-label={`Email ${address}. Opens a new message about a project.`}>{address}</a>

<style>
  /* See the note above: held below 768 until §B.10 round 10's two strings exist. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .plate {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
